// **A shadowing type parameter is a warning, not an error** (E0075).
//
// A method's own `<T>` inside a `class Repo<T>` is a *different* parameter — identity is the
// declaration site, not the spelling — and it shadows the class's, exactly as a local binding
// shadows a global. That is sound and the program means what it says, so this cannot be an error:
// the case below runs, prints, and exits 0 with the warning on the record.
//
// What it costs is legibility, which is what the warning is for. Reading
// `Repo::<Todo>.label::<User>()`, nothing tells you which `T` the body means, and the class's `T`
// is unreachable from that body under any spelling. Rust lints this; C# rejects it outright; here
// it is reported and the program keeps going.
//
// The file also carries the three shapes that must stay **silent**, so a future over-eager version
// of the rule shows up as three extra diagnostics rather than as nothing: a method parameter with a
// different name, a type parameter that merely shares a name with a declared type, and two sibling
// declarations that each spell `T`. (Their gate is `noeta-check`'s unit tests, which assert the
// diagnostic list is *empty*; this corpus header can only assert that expected diagnostics are
// present.)
// expect: error E0075 at 40:14
// expect: stdout "User"
// expect: stdout "Todo"
// expect: stdout "ok"
// expect: exit 0

// Silent: a nominal type may be named `T`. The parameter below does not hide it — a parameter and
// a declared type are different kinds of name, and nobody's `<T>` should have to know what types
// exist elsewhere in the file.
struct T {
    id: int
}
struct Todo {
    id: int
}
struct User {
    id: int
}

class Repo<T> {
    // Warned: this `T` hides the class's, and the two are indistinguishable at any call site.
    fn label<T>(): string {
        return type_name::<T>();
    }

    // Silent: a name of its own, which reaches BOTH parameters — the fix the warning asks for.
    fn distinct<U>(): string {
        return type_name::<T>();
    }
}

// Silent: two sibling declarations may each spell `T`, because neither is inside the other.
fn one<T>(x: T): string {
    return "ok";
}

fn two<T>(x: T): string {
    return "ok";
}

echo Repo::<Todo>.label::<User>();
echo Repo::<Todo>.distinct::<User>();
echo one(1);
